### 11. Current problems and risks

- Shrinking activity. DefiLlama monthly volume fell 73% year on year (October 2025 $12.0B to October 2026 $3.3B), and monthly revenue fell 41% ($2.21M to $1.29M). CoW's own aggregator share dropped from 16.8% (May) to 12.4% (October). Part of the drop was self-inflicted by the CIP-74 volume fee, part was market-wide.

- No value accrual to holders. Buybacks recycle into solver rewards. The burn and buyback-mandate proposals from May 2026 have not been voted. Community members on the forum call the buybacks ineffective for price, and the Core Team itself calls the price "completely disconnected from fundamentals".

- Cost base vs revenue. Service budgets are $12.6M (2026) and $10.45M (2027, proposed), plus solver incentives of about $11.6M per year in COW purchases, against about $15–19M of revenue. The treasury fell 23% in H1 2026. The DAO depends on treasury and COW reserves.

- Buyback pause. No buyback fills have been observed since 2026-10-21, while COW keeps flowing out to solvers: 5.09M in the last 30 days against 2.13M bought. CIP-92 moves buyback duty to the Foundation. Until buybacks resume, solver rewards are net-dilutive.

- Dilution and overhang:

- 121.7M swappable vCOW;

- CIP-83 team stream, with up to 100M COW more in the performance tier;

- grants;

- a dormant 3%-per-year mint right;

- 357M COW in the DAO Safe, which governance can deploy.

- Concentration: about 90% of volume and revenue comes from Ethereum mainnet, and Ethereum-native MEV is the core selling point. The Encrypt-the-Mempool EIP that CoW supports would erode part of the MEV-protection differentiation if adopted.

- Operational and security incidents in 2026: the $50M bad-execution trade in March (reputational damage with Aave) and the DNS hijack in April. The settlement contract itself has never been exploited, and no incident has affected the COW token (Blockworks, as of 2026-09-04).

- Centralization:

- off-chain Snapshot governance;

- a Safe-held treasury;

- a permissioned solver allow-list run by a 4-of-13 Safe (on-chain; Blockworks says 4/15);

- the auction ("autopilot") run on core-team infrastructure;

- reliance on one service provider (Nomev Labs).

- Competition: 0x, KyberSwap, OKX, 1inch, Uniswap (UniswapX and its aggregator), Jupiter and DFlow on Solana, and NEAR Intents. Wallets such as MetaMask run multi-provider quote competitions in which CoW is one of several.

- Partner-fee economics: CIP-75 cut the DAO's share of partner fees to 25%. A December 2025 forum brief criticized this as channeling value to integrators and legal entities.

- Market structure: about $1.4M of 24h volume and 7,391 Ethereum holders, so the token is thinly traded. It is down 95% from its 2022 ATH.

Hype check: there is no strong market narrative behind COW at present. Bullish holders point to revenue at about 3x market cap, embedded-execution partnerships (MetaMask, Lido, Aave, Euler) and AI-agent order flow (x402, MetaMask Agent Wallet). The price action (−52% over one year) shows these have not been priced as catalysts.

