Why the two circulating figures differ. They are about 62.4M HYPE apart. Hyperliquid counts as circulating everything not in the four listed non-circulating addresses or the emission reserve. That includes Hyper Foundation balances and the stake on the Foundation's 5 validators. CoinGecko applies its own exclusions, which it does not publish in its API. Neither figure is "wrong". When computing yields, state which one you use.

Issuance and unlocks.

- Staking emissions. The reward rate is inversely proportional to √(total staked): about 2.37%/yr at 400M staked [4]. The validator API reports a predicted delegator APR of 2.17% for validators charging 3% commission (including all Hyper Foundation validators) and 2.239% for 0%-commission validators, i.e. a gross protocol rate of ~2.24% on 438.7M staked (2026-11-10). The docs' √ formula gives 2.26% at 438.7M, consistent. That implies roughly 9.8M HYPE/yr of new issuance from the future-emissions reserve, about $704M/yr at $71.66. This is an estimate, derived as stake × gross APR. Cross-check: DefiLlama's "Hyper Foundation HYPE Staking" shows $30.94M of rewards over 30 days, which matches 208M Foundation-validator stake × 2.24% × 30/365 ≈ 383k HYPE at ~$80.

- Core contributors. The 238M allocation began distributing on 2025-11-29. Tokenomist models it as 24-month linear vesting of 9,916,666 HYPE/month [30]. Actual distributions out of 0x43e9…a251, read on-chain from userNonFundingLedgerUpdates, have been far below that schedule:

- 2025-11-29: 1,745,745

- 2026-01-06: 1,125,766

- 2026-02-06: 140,333

- 2026-03-07: 173,217

- 2026-04-06: 333,335

- 2026-05-06: 421,879

- 2026-06-06/07: 533,752

- 2026-07-07: 452,000

- 2026-08-06: 433,024

- 2026-09-06: 433,419

- 2026-10-07: 3,750,000 (5 recipients × 750,000)

- 2026-11-06: 179,706

- Total: 9,722,176 HYPE in 12 months.

The vesting address is fully staked and earns staking rewards. That is why its balance, 238.15M, is still above the original 238.0M even after the distributions.

- Ahead. The next contributor distribution is expected around 2026-12-06, based on the monthly pattern. The schedule ceiling is ~9.92M/month until about November 2027. Recent months have been 0.18M–3.75M.

Largest holders and treasuries (on-chain or company-reported):

- Core-contributor vesting address: 238.1M.

- Stake on the 5 Hyper Foundation validators: 54.37M + 52.40M + 51.66M + 34.59M + 15.15M (Hyper Foundation 5) = 208.18M (2026-11-10, validatorSummaries). This is stake delegated to those validators, not necessarily owned by the Foundation.

- Assistance Fund: 48.8M.

- Hyperliquid Strategies Inc (Nasdaq: PURR): ~38.8M HYPE per its company dashboard as of 2026-10-22 (figure as reported in research notes; it does not appear in the cited article [29] and was not re-verified). The "Hyperliquid Strategies x Unit" validator holds 38.94M stake.

- US spot ETFs: 21Shares THYP was the first, launched 2026-05-12 [40]; Bitwise BHYP followed within days and Grayscale HYPG later (exact dates not re-verified). Combined net inflows were ~$172M by 2026-06-16: BHYP ~$106.6M, THYP ~$60M, HYPG ~$8.6M (SoSoValue data via Decrypt) [37]. Current ETF holdings: not available (no fresh primary data retrieved).

### How it works

The chain. Hyperliquid is a custom L1 using HyperBFT consensus, a HotStuff derivative with one-block finality. Execution has two parts [1]:

- HyperCore: a native, fully on-chain central limit order book for perpetual futures and spot, plus margining, liquidations, vaults and staking. It is built into the state machine, not deployed as smart contracts. Docs claim 200k orders/second.

- HyperEVM: an EVM (Cancun, no blobs, EIP-1559) where HYPE is gas. Both base and priority fees are burned. Contracts can read HyperCore state through precompiles and write to it through the CoreWriter system contract 0x3333…3333.

How a trader uses it. A user connects an EVM wallet or logs in by email and deposits USDC (native Circle USDC via CCTP, or the legacy Arbitrum bridge). They trade perps with up to 40x leverage on majors and pay maker/taker fees. There is no gas per order on HyperCore. Funding is paid hourly between longs and shorts. Liquidations are executed by the protocol, and the HLP vault acts as backstop liquidator and market maker. Third-party front-ends ("builders", e.g. Phantom, MetaMask, Based) route orders with "builder codes" and charge their own add-on fee. The docs report >$65M of builder revenue to date [1][43].

Fee schedule [2]:

- Perps base: 0.045% taker / 0.015% maker. Spot base: 0.070% / 0.040%.

- Volume tiers are based on 14-day volume, down to 0.024%/0.000% for perps above $7B.

- HIP-3 markets: the deployer can set an additional deployer fee share of 0–300% of the base fee (0–100% in "growth mode"); if above 100%, the protocol fee rises to match. Growth mode cuts all-in fees (and the protocol's share) by ≥90% for markets disjoint from validator-operated perps (e.g., equities), giving a taker fee of 0.0045%–0.009% [2].

- Maker rebates go up to −0.003%.

- Staking HYPE gives fee discounts: 5% (>10 HYPE) up to 40% (>500k HYPE).

Where fees go (the "fee switch" is already on). The docs say [2][1]: "fees are entirely directed to the community (HLP, the assistance fund, and deployers)." The AF "converts trading fees to HYPE in a fully automated manner as part of the L1 execution. HYPE in the assistance fund is burned." Spot and HIP-3 deployers may keep up to 50% of fees on their own markets. Fees in non-USDC HIP-1 base tokens go to the deployer; the share not redirected is burned. Hyperliquid Labs takes no fee.

Assistance Fund buyback mechanics.

- The AF is a keyless system address. On-chain it shows a continuous stream of small HYPE/USDC spot buys (pair @107), typically hundreds of fills per day, executed passively (fills are mostly crossed=false, fee 0).

- Validators approved treating AF HYPE as burned on 2025-12-24. Reported support was ~85% of stake, with ~37.1M HYPE in the AF when the vote opened on 2025-12-17 (~37M per Tangem) [19][20]. No tokens move; the HYPE simply stays in a system address that cannot spend without a hard fork [18].

- The API still counts AF HYPE inside totalSupply but lists it as non-circulating.

AQAv2 (Aligned Quote Assets v2) — the second revenue leg [5][15]:

- Stablecoin deployers share ~90% of cost-adjusted reserve yield on their Hyperliquid supply with the protocol. For USDC, the treasury deployer is Coinbase and the technical deployer is Circle; each staked 500k HYPE.

- Yield accrues in 30-day intervals from activation (2026-08-26). It is sent to the AF 8 days after each interval ends, from system interest address 0x50…00 + token_index (for USDC, 0x5000000000000000000000000000000000000000).

- The treasury deployer's stake is slashable at 2%/day if the balance is short.

- On-chain:

- Address 0x8536a52900b5e7b23b08d6dcc50fd5689b5e270c funded 0x5000…0000 with 14,580,777 USDC (2026-10-03) and 15,916,963 USDC (2026-11-02).

- 0x5000…0000 forwarded 14,580,775 USDC to the AF at 2026-10-04 00:00 UTC and 15,916,961 USDC at 2026-11-03 00:00 UTC.

- Small 2.0 and 2.52 USDC test sends preceded each.

- Unlike AQAv1 (50% yield share, 200k HYPE stake, fee discounts), AQAv2 gives no trading-fee or volume-contribution benefit. It will become a requirement for quote assets on HIP-4 and validator-operated perps in a future upgrade [5].

- Governance: the AQAv2 validator vote passed on 2026-06-12 with 69.08% of stake (19 of 26 validators voting yes); yield accrual started 2026-08-26 [39][15].

Builder-deployed markets.

- HIP-1: permissionless spot token deployment through a Dutch auction paid in HYPE. Auction proceeds are burned [1].

- HIP-3: anyone staking 500k HYPE can deploy their own perp DEX on HyperCore: equities, indices, commodities, pre-IPO names. The deployer sets oracles and markets, keeps up to 50% of fees, and can be slashed by stake-weighted validator vote (up to 100%; slashed HYPE is burned). The stake must stay in place ≥183 days [3].

- HIP-4 "Outcomes": fully-collateralized binary event contracts on the same engine. Mainnet launched 2026-05-02 [33]; canonical CPI/Fed markets followed in late May 2026 [34].

Staking and governance.

- Delegated proof-of-stake. A validator needs a 10k HYPE self-delegation, locked 1 year. Delegations have a 1-day lockup; unstaking takes 7 days [4].

- Governance is validator-centric: network upgrades need a ≥2/3 stake-weighted vote. Examples: AQAv2 passed with 69.08% [39]; the AF-burn recognition passed with ~85%.

- Delegators "vote" by choosing validators. There is no token-holder DAO contract.

Priority fees. Market makers can bid HYPE for gossip (read) priority in 3-minute Dutch auctions, and pay per-order "order priority" fees for write priority (≈45 ms faster per 1 bp). Both are burned [1].

### Utility

Real, protocol-enforced uses:

- Security / staking: validators and delegators stake HYPE (438.7M staked) for emission rewards of ~2.2% APR. Staking does not pay a share of trading fees.

- Gas: HYPE is the gas token on HyperEVM, where fees are burned, and on Kinetiq's Elysium L2 (mainnet scheduled 2026-11-09) [32]. HyperEVM gas burn is small: DefiLlama "Hyperliquid L1" chain fees were $0.84M over 30 days.

- Permission bonds:

- 500k HYPE to deploy a HIP-3 perp DEX. With ~10 HIP-3 DEXs live, that is ≥5M HYPE locked.

- 500k each for AQAv2 technical and treasury deployers.

- A staking requirement for HIP-4 outcome deployers, which stacks with any HIP-3 stake. The amount is not stated in the docs text reviewed.

- HIP-1 ticker auctions paid in HYPE and burned.

- Trading-fee discounts: 5–40% depending on HYPE staked.

- Latency priority: priority fees paid in HYPE and burned. This is a niche used by market makers.

- Collateral / quote asset: HYPE is a widely used spot pair and DeFi collateral on HyperEVM (lending markets, kHYPE and other LSTs).

What HYPE is NOT needed for:

- Trading perps or spot on Hyperliquid. Users post USDC (or other quote assets) as margin and pay fees in USDC. A trader can use Hyperliquid without ever touching HYPE.

- There is no direct dividend or fee distribution to holders or stakers. Value accrues indirectly through the AF buying HYPE and treating it as burned.

- Governance is not exercised directly by token holders. It goes through the validator set, which is concentrated (see Risks).

### Why people buy and sell it

Demand drivers:

- Cash-flow buyback. About $1.0B/yr annualized flows into HYPE purchases: $829M/yr from the last 30 days of fee revenue, plus ~$180–190M/yr from AQAv2 (derived). Against CoinGecko market cap ($17.15B, 2026-11-10 15:53 UTC) that is a ~5.9% buyback yield; against Hyperliquid-defined circulating cap ($21.63B), ~4.7%. DefiLlama counts 100% of protocol revenue as holders revenue, which is why investors frame HYPE as "exchange equity" with a near-full payout ratio.

- Dominant fee share among perp DEXs. In the last 30 days, Hyperliquid Perps earned $84.9M of fees. The next pure-perp DEXs earned far less: Jupiter Perps $8.2M, Aster $4.1M, Lighter $4.0M (DefiLlama fees overview, 2026-11-10) [10].

- TradFi narratives:

- ICE (NYSE owner) will be exclusive operator of, and take a strategic stake in, trade[XYZ]'s regulated US perp markets built on HIP-3 (2026-10-29; HYPE +13% to ~$91 that day) [21].

- Payward/Kraken plans US perps on Hyperliquid through Bitnomial [24][25].

- Several US spot ETFs exist, including staking (BHYP) [36].

- Corporate treasuries hold HYPE: Hyperliquid Strategies 38.8M.

- New listings: Upbit opened KRW/BTC/USDT pairs on 2026-11-10, 15:00 KST [23].

- Ecosystem utility: HIP-3 stakes, liquid staking (Kinetiq kHYPE), HyperEVM DeFi, the Elysium L2 using HYPE as gas.

Supply / sell pressure:

- Staking emissions of ~9.8M HYPE/yr (estimate) go to stakers, some of whom sell. This includes rewards earned by the staked core-contributor allocation.

- Core-contributor distributions: 9.72M HYPE over the last 12 months, with a schedule ceiling of ~9.92M/month. They follow a monthly rhythm around the 6th–7th. The 2026-10-07 tranche of 3.75M was the largest since vesting began.

- Net supply math (estimate). From 2025-12-17 (AF ≈37.1M) to 2026-11-10 (48.81M), the AF absorbed ~11.7M HYPE. Over a similar window, ~8.0M was distributed to contributors (2026-01-06 to 2026-11-06) and ~9M was emitted to stakers. Gross issuance into hands has therefore been roughly equal to or above buybacks at current prices. HYPE is not yet net-deflationary on a circulating basis.

- Leverage: HYPE itself is a heavily traded perp on Hyperliquid, with $1.53B open interest and $403M 24h volume on 2026-11-10 15:55 UTC. That makes it prone to liquidation-driven moves.

- Corporate treasury discount: PURR traded below NAV and its board expanded share buybacks to $100M (2026-10-23) [29]. This signals treasury-company demand for HYPE has stalled.

### The protocol/business

What it does. Hyperliquid aims to be "the liquidity infrastructure" for on-chain finance [1]. Product lines:

- Validator-operated perps. 179 active perp markets on the main DEX (235 in the universe, of which 56 are delisted). On 2026-11-10 15:55 UTC: open interest $8.85B, 24h volume $6.22B (an earlier ~14:30 UTC read gave $9.17B / $5.29B). Top markets by 24h volume: BTC $3.11B, ETH $1.18B, ZEC $0.53B, HYPE $0.40B, SOL $0.27B. Max leverage 40x (BTC).

- HIP-3 builder perps (equities, indices, commodities, pre-IPO). 10 deployed DEXs [6]. Activity is extremely concentrated in trade[XYZ] (xyz): 144 markets, $4.18B open interest and $4.91B 24h volume on 2026-11-10 15:55 UTC. Top markets by volume: XYZ100 $0.59B, NVDA $0.48B, SKHX $0.46B, SP500 $0.46B. EntropyIO (io) had $207M OI and $166M volume, mostly the ANTH (Anthropic pre-IPO) perp ($176M OI). Paragon had $16M OI. Felix, Ventuals, HyENA, Markets by Kinetiq (km and mkts), ABCDEx and dreamcash showed ≤$0.2M OI at the time of the read.

- Spot order book. HIP-1 tokens plus bridged assets (Unit BTC/ETH/SOL etc.). Spot fees were $2.65M over 30 days.

- HIP-4 Outcomes (prediction markets). 30-day fees $0.15M; still tiny.

- HLP vault. A protocol market-making and liquidation vault: account value $149.5M on 2026-11-10, 30-day PnL +$2.14M, all-time PnL +$140.8M.

- HyperEVM. General-purpose DeFi: lending (HyperLend, Felix), LSTs (Kinetiq, Hyperbeat), DEXs (HyperSwap etc.).

- Stablecoin rails. $6.25B native USDC totalSupply on HyperEVM (2026-11-10 15:55 UTC; $6.30B at ~14:30 UTC; contract 0xb88339CB7199b77E23DB6E890353E22632Ba630f, a Circle FiatTokenProxy per Etherscan V2). The legacy Arbitrum bridge 0x2Df1…3dF7 holds $116.28M USDC (15:55 UTC). Native Markets' USDH and USDT0 are also listed.

Totals (aggregation of the info API, 2026-11-10 15:55 UTC): perp open interest ≈ $13.25B (main $8.85B + HIP-3 $4.40B) and 24h perp volume ≈ $11.31B (main $6.22B + HIP-3 $5.09B). HIP-3 markets are ~45% of daily perp volume. OI here is open interest in coins × mark price; Castle Labs reports a lower $6.90B for Hyperliquid with its own method [38]. DefiLlama TVL is $7.69B on "Hyperliquid L1" plus $0.12B on Arbitrum (2026-11-10) [9].

Audience and where it is popular.

- The core audience is crypto-native active traders and market makers: high-leverage retail, "whales" whose positions are publicly visible, and professional HFT firms using priority fees. Hyperliquid self-reported 1,199,467 cumulative addresses and 36k–60k daily active traders in late March–early April 2026 (stats endpoint, last updated 2026-04-03) [14].

- Asian retail is a large constituency. Korea is now served by Upbit's HYPE listing; there are Chinese-language communities and validators (HyperCN).

- US persons are restricted from the main front-end. The US-regulated wrappers (ICE, Payward) are pending CFTC approval.

- HIP-3 equity perps attract traders seeking 24/7 leveraged exposure to US tech stocks and indices.

Market share vs competitors (sources disagree by method):

| Metric | Hyperliquid | Competitors | As of / source |

| --- | --- | --- | --- |

| 30-day fees | $84.9M (Perps) + $2.65M (Spot) | Jupiter Perps $8.2M; Aster Perps $4.1M; Lighter Perps $4.0M (+$2.3M Lighter Robinhood); edgeX V2 $2.0M; dYdX v4 $0.24M | 2026-11-10, DefiLlama fees overview [10] |

| 24h perp volume | $10.14B, 44% of $22.97B tracked on-chain perp volume | Lighter $2.96B (13%); Variational $2.09B (9.1%); Aster $1.82B (7.9%) | Castle Labs perp DEX rankings, updated 2026-11-10 [38] |

| Open interest share | 56% of tracked on-chain perp OI ($6.90B of $12.41B) | Aster 11%; Variational 6.7%; Lighter 5.6% (≈23% combined) | Castle Labs, 2026-11-10 [38] |

Why the numbers diverge: Lighter's retail trades are zero-fee and some competitors' volume is incentive-farmed, so fee share overstates and volume share understates Hyperliquid's economic dominance. Whether HIP-3 volume is counted also changes Hyperliquid's share by up to ~2x.

Key integrations and partners:

- Circle (native USDC, CCTP; AQAv2 technical deployer) and Coinbase (AQAv2 treasury deployer) [15].

- ICE / trade[XYZ] [21]; Payward-Kraken / Bitnomial [24].

- Front-ends using builder codes: Phantom, MetaMask, Based and others [43].

- Unit (bridged BTC/ETH/SOL); Kinetiq (LST, Elysium L2).

- Institutional validators: Anchorage by Figment, Nansen x HypurrCollective, Hyperliquid Strategies x Unit.

- Bitwise, 21Shares and Grayscale ETFs.

### Revenue and value capture

(a) Gross fees paid by users and (b) protocol revenue (DefiLlama, slug hyperliquid; days are UTC; last complete day 2026-11-09):

| Period | Fees (users paid) | Revenue = Holders revenue (to AF) | Revenue / fees |

| --- | --- | --- | --- |

| 24h (2026-11-09) | $3,376,496 | $2,525,577 | 75% |

| 7d (2026-11-03..09) | $21,102,471 | $16,059,203 | 76% |

| 30d (2026-10-11..11-09) | $88,228,442 | $68,165,919 | 77% |

| Annualized from 30d (×365/30) | ~$1.073B | ~$829M | — |

| Trailing 1y | $895,451,627 | $681,194,522 | 76% |

| All-time | $1,740,113,133 | $1,405,071,910 | 81% |

The 2026-11-09 fee breakdown was Perps $3.26M, Spot $0.087M, HLP $0.020M and Outcomes $0.008M. The ~23–25% gap between fees and revenue is the HIP-3 deployer share (trade[XYZ] alone shows $9.07M of 30-day fees as an "Interface" on DefiLlama), spot-deployer shares, referral rebates and HLP. DefiLlama reports revenue = holders revenue, i.e. all protocol revenue goes to HYPE via the AF.

Monthly fees and revenue (DefiLlama, $M):

| Month | 2025-10 | 2025-11 | 2025-12 | 2026-01 | 2026-02 | 2026-03 | 2026-04 | 2026-05 | 2026-06 | 2026-07 | 2026-08 | 2026-09 | 2026-10 |

| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |

| Fees | 124.8 | 101.4 | 68.7 | 77.3 | 70.7 | 69.5 | 58.7 | 62.2 | 81.0 | 55.1 | 77.8 | 91.3 | 88.0 |

| Revenue | 97.2 | 77.7 | 51.2 | 59.8 | 54.0 | 51.5 | 42.4 | 46.3 | 60.0 | 38.4 | 61.4 | 74.2 | 69.1 |

(c) What actually reaches token holders:

- Trading-fee buybacks. The protocol credits fee USDC to the AF system address 0xfefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefe as part of L1 execution. There is no smart-contract function to call; it is native state-machine logic. The AF then buys HYPE on the HYPE/USDC spot book (@107). On-chain read: between 2026-10-30 01:21 UTC and 2026-11-10 15:52 UTC, the AF executed 10,278 buy fills (all on @107) for 438,821 HYPE costing $36.41M (avg $82.97). Its balance went from 48,367,210 to 48,805,892 HYPE over the same fills (a first read to 14:50 UTC gave 10,215 fills, 433,141 HYPE, $36.00M). That figure includes the 2026-11-03 deployment of the AQAv2 payment ($17.45M of buys that day). Excluding 2026-11-03, AF buys for 2026-11-04..09 were $11.78M, against DefiLlama revenue of $13.99M for the same UTC days. The AF lags slightly because it executes passively.

- AQAv2 USDC reserve yield: $14,580,775 (2026-10-04) and $15,916,961 (2026-11-03). That is ~$15M per 30-day interval, ≈$180–190M/yr if USDC supply and rates stay level. This is a run-rate, not guidance; Hyperliquid has not published a forecast [15][17]. DefiLlama's Hyperliquid fees/revenue series does not appear to include AQAv2. Its 30-day revenue is fee-only, so total value to holders over the last 30 days was ≈ $68.2M + $15.9M ≈ $84.1M.

- USDH (AQAv1): Native Markets said 50% of USDH reserve yield goes to the AF [18]. USDH is small: the AF held 43,220 USDH. Amount not separately quantified.

- Direct burns (removed from totalSupply): 1,236,100 HYPE cumulative as of 2026-11-10 15:53 UTC (1B − 998,763,900), from deploy auctions, priority fees, HyperEVM gas and unrouted spot fees.

- Staking rewards are not revenue. The ~2.2% APR is new issuance from the emissions reserve, i.e. dilution of non-stakers.

- Cross-check of cumulative capture. The AF's on-chain entry notional is $1,430,880,978 for 48.81M HYPE (avg ≈ $29.32; 2026-11-10 15:53 UTC). DefiLlama's all-time holders revenue (through 2026-11-09) is $1,405,071,910. The ~$26M gap is consistent with the $30.5M of AQAv2 USDC (not in DefiLlama) minus small definitional differences. The two independent sources agree within ~2%.

### How to verify on-chain

All Hyperliquid info calls are POST https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info, need no key, and return JSON.

1. Supply, circulating, non-circulating, future emissions. Values obtained on 2026-11-10: total 998,764,836.7; circulating 301,779,770.3; futureEmissions 410,035,672.4 (~14:30 UTC). Fact-check re-run at 16:00 UTC: 998,763,867.5; 301,774,019.1; 410,034,440.0.

curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"type":"tokenDetails","tokenId":"0x0d01dc56dcaaca66ad901c959b4011ec"}' \ | jq '{totalSupply,circulatingSupply,futureEmissions,nonCirculatingUserBalances,markPx}'

2. Assistance Fund HYPE balance and average entry. Got 48,800,351.12 HYPE, entryNtl $1,430,469,298 (~14:30 UTC); re-run at 16:00 UTC: 48,806,365.52 HYPE, entryNtl $1,430,904,938.

curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"type":"spotClearinghouseState","user":"0xfefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefe"}' \ | jq '.balances[] | select(.coin=="HYPE" or .coin=="USDC")'

3. Assistance Fund buybacks (fills). The API keeps only the most recent ~10,000 fills and returns at most 2,000 per call; paginate by setting startTime to the last fill time + 1. The single call below (from 2026-11-04 00:00 UTC) returned one page worth $9.55M at 16:00 UTC, not the full period. Sum sz*px per day. @107 = HYPE/USDC.

curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"type":"userFillsByTime","user":"0xfefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefe","startTime":1793750400000,"aggregateByTime":true}' \ | jq '[.[] | (.sz|tonumber)*(.px|tonumber)] | add'

4. AQAv2 USDC inflows to the AF. Look for send deltas from 0x5000000000000000000000000000000000000000; got 14,580,774.69 USDC on 2026-10-04 and 15,916,960.66 USDC on 2026-11-03.

curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"type":"userNonFundingLedgerUpdates","user":"0xfefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefefe","startTime":1790000000000}' \ | jq '.[] | select(.delta.token=="USDC") | {time,from:.delta.user,amount:.delta.amount}'

5. Core-contributor distributions. Filter spotTransfer outflows from the vesting address; got 179,706 HYPE on 2026-11-06. Use delegatorSummary for the staked balance (238,147,366).

curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"type":"userNonFundingLedgerUpdates","user":"0x43e9abea1910387c4292bca4b94de81462f8a251","startTime":1793000000000}' \ | jq '.[] | select(.delta.type=="spotTransfer") | {time,amt:.delta.amount,to:.delta.destination}'

6. Staking totals and APR. Stake is in 1e-8 units; got 438,718,042 total. APR is in .stats[0][1].predictedApr: 2.17% (3% commission) to 2.24% (0% commission). Filter .name for "Hyper Foundation" to see the 5 Foundation validators.

curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"type":"validatorSummaries"}' \ | jq '[.[].stake] | add / 1e8'

7. Volume and open interest (main DEX + HIP-3 DEXs). List the HIP-3 DEXs with {"type":"perpDexs"}.

curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"type":"metaAndAssetCtxs"}' \ | jq '[.[1][] | (.dayNtlVlm|tonumber)] | add'curl -s https://api.hyperliquid.xyz/info -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"type":"metaAndAssetCtxs","dex":"xyz"}' \ | jq '[.[1][] | (.openInterest|tonumber)*(.markPx|tonumber)] | add'

8. HyperEVM reads (public RPC https://rpc.hyperliquid.xyz/evm, chain id 999). WHYPE totalSupply: got 3,213,585.69 (~14:30 UTC) and 3,221,291.17 (15:55 UTC). Native USDC totalSupply: got 6,296,819,256.89 (~14:30 UTC) and 6,252,895,554.77 (15:55 UTC). Divide the hex result by 1e18 (WHYPE) or 1e6 (USDC).

curl -s https://rpc.hyperliquid.xyz/evm -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"eth_call","params":[{"to":"0x5555555555555555555555555555555555555555","data":"0x18160ddd"},"latest"]}'curl -s https://rpc.hyperliquid.xyz/evm -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"eth_call","params":[{"to":"0xb88339CB7199b77E23DB6E890353E22632Ba630f","data":"0x18160ddd"},"latest"]}'# Etherscan V2 equivalent (key not shown):curl -s "https://api.etherscan.io/v2/api?chainid=999&module=stats&action=tokensupply&contractaddress=0x5555555555555555555555555555555555555555&apikey=$ETHERSCAN_API_KEY"

9. Legacy Arbitrum bridge USDC. Got 116,973,365 USDC (~14:30 UTC) and 116,281,321 USDC (15:55 UTC; RPC and Etherscan V2 chainid=42161 agree).

curl -s https://arbitrum-one-rpc.publicnode.com -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"eth_call","params":[{"to":"0xaf88d065e77c8cC2239327C5EDb3A432268e5831","data":"0x70a082310000000000000000000000002df1c51e09aecf9cacb7bc98cb1742757f163df7"},"latest"]}'

10. DefiLlama fee/revenue series (second source). AQAv2 is not included.

curl -s "https://api.llama.fi/summary/fees/hyperliquid?dataType=dailyFees" | jq '{total24h,total7d,total30d,total1y,totalAllTime}'curl -s "https://api.llama.fi/summary/fees/hyperliquid?dataType=dailyHoldersRevenue" | jq '{total24h,total7d,total30d,total1y,totalAllTime}'

Explorers for manual inspection: hypurrscan.io (HyperCore addresses, e.g. /address/0xfefe…fefe) and hyperevmscan.io (HyperEVM). Note: DefiLlama's /summary/derivatives (volume) endpoint now returns HTTP 402 (paid plan) as of 2026-11-10. Use the Hyperliquid info API for volume and OI instead.

### Key people

- Jeff Yan — co-founder; leads Hyperliquid Labs (titled Co-Founder & CEO in some profiles). Previously at Hudson River Trading, then ran the crypto market-making firm Chameleon Trading. Harvard. X: [@chameleon_jeff](https://x.com/chameleon_jeff). He is the public face. In October 2026 he told Bloomberg that US regulators should oversee firms building markets on Hyperliquid rather than the chain itself [28][42].

- iliensinc (pseudonymous) — co-founder, Harvard classmate of Yan. Co-leads Hyperliquid Labs [1]. X: @iliensinc.

- Hyperliquid Labs — the core development company. Small: about 11–12 people per press profiles (not confirmed by Labs). Self-funded, no external capital. Team members are from Caltech/MIT; ex-Airtable, Citadel, HRT, Nuro [1].

- Hyper Foundation — the ecosystem/governance foundation. X: [@HyperFND](https://x.com/HyperFND). Runs 5 validators with 208.18M HYPE delegated (47.5% of stake, 2026-11-10). Proposes validator votes (e.g., the AF-burn recognition) and funds grants (e.g., 1M HYPE to the Hyperliquid Policy Center). Individual Foundation officers are not publicly named in the sources reviewed.

- Official accounts: [@HyperliquidX](https://x.com/HyperliquidX) (protocol).

- Hyperliquid Policy Center (Washington DC, launched 2026-02-18) — CEO Jake Chervinsky (ex-Blockchain Association, Variant); Policy Counsel Brad Bourque; Policy Director Salah Ghazzal [35].

- Adjacent (not Hyperliquid entities): Hyperliquid Strategies Inc (Nasdaq: PURR), a HYPE treasury company running a validator with Unit; trade[XYZ], the largest HIP-3 deployer, now partnered with ICE (CEO Jeffrey Sprecher) [21]; Payward/Kraken (Jon Pham, head of US derivatives) [25]; Kinetiq (kHYPE, Elysium L2).

- Departures: no publicly reported departures of named Hyperliquid Labs leaders were found in the last 12 months.

### Recent news (last ~90 days)

- 2026-08-19 — President Trump said CFTC Chair Michael Selig was working to bring Hyperliquid into the US "in a fully compliant and legal fashion"; HYPE rose ~11% (secondary report) [42].

- 2026-08-24 — Kinetiq announces Elysium, an L2 settling to HyperEVM with HYPE as gas [32].

- 2026-08-26 — AQAv2 yield accrual starts for USDC, with Coinbase as treasury deployer and Circle as technical deployer. The validator vote had passed on 2026-06-12 with 69.08% of stake [15][39].

- 2026-08-31 — Bloomberg reports advanced talks between Hyperliquid and Kraken-parent Payward on US perps [24]. On 2026-09-16 Payward announced plans for HIP-3 perps for eligible US clients via its CFTC-regulated Bitnomial [25]; on 2026-10-26 Hyperliquid named Payward its intended first CFTC-regulated HIP-3 deployer, with Bitnomial (DCM/DCO) creating and clearing the markets and NinjaTrader Clearing as FCM, subject to regulatory approval [26].

- 2026-09-23 — HYPE all-time high $97.96 (CoinGecko).

- 2026-09-29 — House Oversight Chairman James Comer sends Hyperliquid Labs a records request (deadline 2026-10-13). It is part of an insider-trading probe into prediction/trading platforms, citing a ~$1.1B leveraged BTC/ETH short. It is a letter, not a subpoena; no findings [27].

- 2026-10-04 — First AQAv2 payment: 14.58M USDC to the AF, deployed into HYPE over the following ~48h [16].

- 2026-10-07 — Largest core-contributor distribution since vesting began: 3.75M HYPE (on-chain).

- 2026-10-23 — Hyperliquid Strategies (PURR) raises its share-buyback authorization from $30M to $100M (to 2027-10-31). Shares had fallen >30% from their October high; its HYPE holding was reported at ~38.8M (not in the cited article) [29].

- 2026-10-29 — ICE signs an agreement to be exclusive operator of trade[XYZ]'s regulated US perp markets and takes a minority strategic stake (terms undisclosed). Subject to regulatory (CFTC) approval; no launch date. HYPE +13% to ~$91 (intraday high $92.10 on KuCoin) [21][22][44].

- 2026-11-03 — Second AQAv2 payment: 15.92M USDC to the AF (on-chain; funded by 0x8536…270c on 2026-11-02).

- 2026-11-06 — Core-contributor distribution of 179,706 HYPE, against a ~9.92M modeled schedule [31] (on-chain confirmed).

- 2026-11-09 — Elysium L2 mainnet scheduled launch date [32]. Live status not independently verified.

- 2026-11-10 — Upbit lists HYPE (KRW, BTC, USDT pairs; HyperEVM network only), trading from 15:00 KST [23].

### Current problems and risks

- Price momentum and revenue off peak.

- HYPE is −27% from its 2026-09-23 ATH and −16.4% over 30 days, though +73% over 1 year (CoinGecko 2026-11-10 15:53 UTC). It fell ~21% from ~$91 on 2026-10-29 to $71.66.

- Monthly fees in 2026 ($55–91M) have not regained Oct–Nov 2025 levels ($125M / $101M).

- Daily fees are volatile: $1.24M on 2026-11-07 vs $4.92M on 2026-11-04.

- Dilution roughly offsets buybacks. Staking issuance (~9.8M HYPE/yr, est.) plus contributor distributions (9.7M over 12 months; up to ~9.92M/month allowed) exceed AF absorption (~11.7M HYPE since 2025-12-17). There are 238.1M HYPE still in the contributor address, worth ~$17.1B at $71.66. That is a long-dated overhang, even though actual monthly releases have been small.

- Validator concentration. The 5 Hyper Foundation validators hold ~208.2M of 438.7M staked (47.5%, 2026-11-10), which exceeds the 1/3 needed to block any upgrade. 27 validators are active. Governance outcomes (AF burn recognition, AQAv2) were Foundation-proposed and passed by stake-weighted votes.

- Concentration in one HIP-3 deployer. trade[XYZ] provides ~$4.91B of ~$11.3B daily perp volume and $4.18B of ~$13.25B OI (2026-11-10 15:55 UTC). It keeps up to 50% of fees on its markets, so this volume is worth less per dollar to HYPE than core perps. Its US expansion now depends on ICE and the CFTC. Several other HIP-3 DEXs showed zero OI.

- Regulatory exposure. The House Oversight records request (2026-09-29) is unresolved publicly. The US path depends on CFTC approvals for ICE/trade[XYZ] and Payward/Bitnomial. Equity perps on HIP-3 sit in a grey zone with the SEC/CFTC.

- Competition. Lighter (zero-fee retail), Aster, edgeX and Variational have taken volume share. Castle Labs puts Hyperliquid at 44% of tracked on-chain perp 24h volume and 56% of OI on 2026-11-10; tracked on-chain OI fell 8.7% over 30 days [38]. Fee dominance (≈10x the next venue) is intact.

- Market-structure and tail risk. High leverage, publicly visible whale positions, past HLP losses from manipulated or illiquid markets (e.g., JELLY in March 2025), and auto-deleveraging during the 2025-10-10 crash. HLP is now relatively small ($149.5M account value), which limits its loss-absorbing capacity.

- AQAv2 is rate-sensitive. The ~$15M/month depends on USDC balances on Hyperliquid ($6.25B native USDC, 2026-11-10) and US T-bill rates. Fed cuts or USDC outflows reduce it directly.

- Treasury-company and ETF demand fading. PURR trades below NAV and is buying back stock rather than HYPE [29]. Fresh ETF flow data was not available for this report.

What the market believes in (bull case, with evidence):

- Hyperliquid becomes the venue layer for 24/7 perps on everything: equities, indices, pre-IPO, events. It is distributed through regulated US intermediaries (ICE, Payward) while fees keep flowing to HYPE.

- Evidence: equity perps on trade[XYZ] already rival crypto majors (NVDA ~$0.48B, SP500 ~$0.46B 24h volume on 2026-11-10); a NYSE-parent partnership; AQAv2 adding a non-trading revenue stream; Upbit listing; 100% of DefiLlama-counted protocol revenue programmatically buying HYPE.

