### How it works

#### The product

Aave is a non-custodial, over-collateralized money market.

- Suppliers deposit assets into a pool and receive interest-bearing aTokens, whose balance grows every block.

- Borrowers post collateral and borrow other assets at a variable rate. The rate follows a utilization curve, with a kink at the "optimal utilization" point.

- Liquidations: if a borrower's health factor falls below 1, anyone can repay part of the debt and seize collateral at a bonus, typically 4.5–6%.

- Reserve factor: a share of all interest goes to the DAO treasury (the Collector). On-chain values on V3 Ethereum Core as of 2026-11-10: USDC 10%, USDT 10%, WETH 15%, wstETH 35%, WBTC 50%, GHO 100%.

- Liquidation protocol fee: 10–20% of the liquidation bonus also goes to the treasury.

- Flash loans charge a fee.

- Chainlink SVR: since 2025, Chainlink Smart Value Recapture returns MEV from Ethereum liquidations to the Collector.

#### Versions

- Aave V3 is the workhorse: $15.10B TVL and $11.23B borrowed (DL). It runs on about 20 networks, including Ethereum Core/Prime, Base, Arbitrum, Avalanche, Plasma, Monad, Mantle, BNB Chain, X Layer, Polygon, Linea and MegaETH.

- Aave V4 launched on Ethereum on 2026-03-30 ([changelog](https://aave.com/docs/resources/changelog)).

- Design: "hub-and-spoke". Liquidity Hubs hold the deposits, and Spokes are isolated markets with their own collateral rules. Spokes can be run by external curators: Sentora's curated hub went live in October 2026, with the DAO receiving 50% of instance revenue.

- New feature: a "Risk Premium" that prices borrow rates by collateral quality.

- Deployments: Avalanche on 2026-07-15, Arc (Circle's chain) on 2026-09-16, and an Equities Hub with a MAG7 Spoke on Base on 2026-10-07 ([changelog](https://aave.com/docs/resources/changelog); the Snapshot vote to deploy V4 on Base closed on 2026-09-25).

- Size: DL shows V4 at $0.79B net TVL and $0.52B borrowed. Aave Labs reports more than $1.5B in deposits and more than $450M in loans ([AL October update](https://governance.aave.com/t/al-development-update-october-2026/25979)). The two figures differ because DL counts deposits net of borrows.

- Aave Horizon is a permissioned RWA market with $0.22B TVL (DL).

- Aave V2 is legacy: $0.10B TVL, being wound down.

#### GHO

GHO is Aave's own over-collateralized stablecoin. Borrowers mint it against Aave collateral, and 100% of GHO borrow interest goes to the DAO. Its peg is defended by the GHO Stability Module (1:1 swaps with USDC and USDT) and by a savings rate (sGHO). As of 2026-11-10:

- The on-chain GHO totalSupply() on Ethereum is 699.0M. This includes GHO minted by DAO-controlled facilitators into Aave pools, so the user float is lower.

- CG prices GHO at $0.9981.

- On 2026-10-29 GHO briefly traded at $0.997 as holders redeemed 4.6M GHO through the stability module ([LlamaRisk](https://governance.aave.com/t/binance-hot-wallet-exploit-aaves-response/25954)).

#### Safety: Umbrella and the Safety Module

Since 2025, first-loss cover for bad debt comes from "Umbrella". In Umbrella, users stake aTokens or GHO into per-asset vaults that can be burned automatically to cover a deficit in that same reserve. Umbrella stake balances on 2026-11-10 (on-chain totalAssets(), in units of the staked wrapper — waUSDC, waUSDT, waWETH static aTokens — not of the plain underlying; re-read by the fact-checker with identical results):

- stkwaUSDC: 46.07M

- stkwaUSDT: 49.80M

- stkwaWETH: 10,463 WETH

- stkGHO: 1.10M

Every vault's exchange rate is still exactly 1:1, so none has been slashed. During the rsETH incident, Aave Labs proposed pausing stkwaWETH as a precaution ([forum](https://governance.aave.com/t/direct-to-aip-pause-stkwaweth-umbrella-staked-token-on-ethereum-v3/24595)). The vault is unpaused now.

Legacy stkAAVE is no longer a slashable backstop. On-chain getMaxSlashablePercentage() returns 0, the cooldown is 2 days (172,800 s) and the unstake window is 2 days. stkAAVE now works as a governance-plus-yield wrapper funded from the Ecosystem Reserve.

#### Governance

- Voting: AAVE, stkAAVE and aAAVE holders vote on-chain through Aave Governance V3 ([0x9AEE0B04…2BC7](https://etherscan.io/address/0x9AEE0B04504CeF83A65AC3f0e838D0593BCb2BC7)), with voting possible on L2s. Off-chain sentiment votes happen on Snapshot.

- Process: Governance Framework v2 was proposed on 2026-07-20. It drops the mandatory TEMP CHECK and shortens the process from 19 days to 13 ([ARFC](https://governance.aave.com/t/arfc-governance-framework-v2/25348)).

- Fast actors: a Protocol Guardian multisig can freeze or pause reserves. Risk Stewards make bounded cap and rate changes daily.

- Fee switch: there is no fee switch to holders. Revenue accrues to the DAO treasury, and governance decides how to spend it.

### Utility

- Real utility:

- Governance. AAVE controls a treasury of about $260M plus all protocol parameters, listings, fees and service-provider budgets. After "Aave Will Win" it also controls the revenue of Aave-branded products.

- Staking yield of about 2.2% APR in AAVE, paid from the treasury's AAVE, not from fees.

- Collateral: AAVE is listed as collateral on Aave V3. About 797K AAVE is supplied on V3 Ethereum.

- Not needed for:

- Using Aave. Supplying, borrowing, paying interest and minting GHO need no AAVE.

- Gas.

- Fee discounts. Since Umbrella replaced stkAAVE, the former stkAAVE GHO-borrow discount and slashing role no longer matter for users.

- Economic claim: there is no legal or automatic claim on cash flow. Value accrual depends entirely on governance choosing buybacks or distributions. The DAO did this in 2025–26 and has paused it since 2026-04-19.

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- "Blue-chip DeFi cash-flow" thesis. Aave is the largest lender, with $615.9M of fees and $87.7M of DAO revenue over the trailing 365 days (DL). Since the April 2026 "Aave Will Win" vote, 100% of protocol, GHO and product revenue is routed to the DAO.

- Buyback and burn expectations. Stani Kulechov said on 2026-06-25 that Aavenomics 3.0 will have "immutable and automated buybacks", and on 2026-09-28 that a burn is being considered. AAVE rose about 11% on the burn remark ([TechFlow/KuCoin](https://www.kucoin.com/news/flash/aave-considers-token-burn-pump-continues-buybacks-where-do-repurchased-tokens-go)).

- Institutional and RWA narratives: Horizon RWA, tokenized stocks on Base, Circle's Arc, Babylon native-BTC collateral (approved 2026-11-09), and the Avon acquisition for fixed-rate lending (2026-11-06).

- Strategic buyers. Kraken parent Payward disclosed open-market AAVE purchases of undisclosed size on 2026-11-05 ([Crypto Briefing](https://cryptobriefing.com/payward-acquires-aave-tokens-defi-push/)). Earlier, in June 2026, CoinDesk reported talks about 250K AAVE plus 15% of Aave Group for 35,000 ETH. Kulechov denied that framing.

- Regulatory tailwind. The SEC closed its four-year probe in December 2025 with no action (reported by Kulechov on 2025-12-16; no primary link was re-checked by the fact-checker). In October/November 2026, Aave filed a framework with the SEC arguing that DeFi lending is not a security.

#### Sell pressure and supply

- Treasury transfers: 150 AAVE/day of stkAAVE emissions, plus AAVE paid to service providers and Aave Labs (75K AAVE grant). These are funded from the 559K Ecosystem Reserve.

- Aave Labs' undisclosed allocation, which is openly being shopped to partners.

- Event-driven dumping. The 2026-10-29 Binance hot-wallet exploiter sold AAVE on DEXs, and AAVE fell about 9% within hours (LlamaRisk). During the rsETH crisis, AAVE fell 17.7% on the day.

- Revenue decline and buyback pause since April 2026. DL holder revenue over the last 30 days is $0, and tokenbuybacks.app shows no buyback since 2026-04-19.

- Contributor exodus and governance conflict. BGD Labs (April 2026), Chaos Labs (April 2026) and ACI (about July 2026) all left. There were also disputes over the Labs budget.

### The protocol/business

Aave is a multi-chain lending marketplace. Its customers are:

- Crypto-native leverage users: ETH/LST looping, basis trades with sUSDe/PT tokens.

- Stablecoin yield seekers.

- Funds and treasuries.

- Integrators that route deposits into Aave, such as Privy Earn, fintech and neobank "earn" products, wallets and CoW Swap.

- Increasingly, permissioned institutions on Horizon and Circle Mint customers borrowing USDC against cirBTC.

Usage is overwhelmingly on Ethereum mainnet. Per DL, Ethereum accounts for $13.41B of the $16.22B TVL and $30.6M of the $39.3M 30-day fees. Secondary chains by 30-day fees are Plasma ($2.96M), Monad ($1.23M), Base ($1.18M), Arbitrum ($0.90M) and Avalanche ($0.74M).

Product lines:

- Aave V3 and V4 protocol

- GHO stablecoin

- Horizon (RWA)

- Aave App, a consumer savings app in invite-based early access ("Ghost Pass"), expanding through September–October 2026

- Aave Pro (V4 interface)

- Aave Kit (B2B)

- Swaps on aave.com via CoW Swap partner fees

- Aave Card (planned)

Aave Labs reports $1.1T of all-time borrow volume (October 2026).

