Upcoming unlocks:

- DefiLlama model: the unlocked amount rises from ~663.8M today to ~716.3M by 2027-05-09 and ~769.3M by 2027-11-10 (re-computed 2026-11-10). That is about +105M MORPHO (~$211M at $2.00) over the next 12 months:

- users and distributors: +65M (DAO and Association incentive and grant flows);

- strategic partners: +31M (Cohort 3, about 2.8M a month until 21 Nov 2027);

- contributors: +8M.

- Founders, sources disagree: DefiLlama treats the founders' 152M as fully unlocked. The docs say founders re-locked into "an additional 2 year linear vest, following a 1 year lockup from the earliest of any future transferability date or May 17th 2025". Transferability (21 Nov 2024) came first, so the formula implies a lockup to 21 Nov 2025 and linear vesting to about 21 Nov 2027, roughly 6.3M a month (my reading; the docs only state the outer bound of 17 May 2028). The docs are the primary source but give only an outer bound, so treat the founders' remaining vesting as uncertain.

- New grant: on 2026-04-30 the DAO executed MIP-131, a 150M MORPHO grant to the Association for 2026–2030 (tx 0x95cc7c983117f1e48423cc1cfebadd0397323273438f52bff2949c6a1b5fd571). Since then the Association has sent tens of millions of MORPHO to unlabeled addresses, for example two transfers of 10.45M each on 2026-05-06 (to the same address 0xab33…f0d9) and two transfers totalling 9.52M (4.35M + 5.18M) on 2026-09-16. In total the Association sent out about 75.6M wrapped MORPHO (gross, 183 transfers) between 2026-04-30 and 2026-11-09, against 150.5M received (Etherscan V2 tokentx, my query 2026-11-10). The $175M round on 2026-06-09 was a token sale, but these transfers are not labeled on-chain, so I cannot confirm they went to round investors.

### How it works

#### Product mechanics

- Morpho Blue (variable rate), singleton contract 0xBBBBBbbBBb9cC5e90e3b3Af64bdAF62C37EEFFCb on Ethereum, Base and other chains. It is an immutable contract where anyone can create an isolated market with one loan asset, one collateral asset, an oracle, an interest rate model (IRM) and a liquidation LTV (LLTV). Only governance-whitelisted LLTVs and IRMs can be used. Lenders earn the interest borrowers pay, and liquidators earn a liquidation incentive. Bad debt stays inside the affected market and does not spread across a shared pool as it can at Aave. Example: the largest listed market, cbBTC/USDC on Base (86% LLTV), had $1.888B supplied, $1.619B borrowed and a 5.46% borrow APY on 2026-11-10 (Morpho API and on-chain market() read, Base block 55,680,727).

- Morpho Vaults (V1 "MetaMorpho" and V2) are ERC-4626 vaults. A curator sets which markets a vault may lend to and the caps; an allocator moves funds between them. Depositors get one token and passive yield. Curators charge performance and/or management fees to depositors; the protocol takes nothing. Risk-increasing changes are timelocked so depositors can exit first. More than 30 curators are active, including Steakhouse, Gauntlet, Sentora, Galaxy, Bitwise, Armitage (Wintermute), Re7, kpk and Spark ([Sept 2026 newsletter](https://morpho.org/blog/the-morpho-effect-september-2026)).

- Morpho Midnight (fixed rate, fixed term):

- Launched on Base in July 2026, then Ethereum (Sept 2026); P2P orders are available on Ethereum, Base, Arc and Robinhood Chain (Morpho newsletters, Oct and Nov 2026).

- Lenders and borrowers trade "units" that mature at a fixed date, and the unit price implies the rate (r = 1/P − 1). The model is an order book or offer system with P2P orders and multi-collateral support (up to 128 collateral configs per market).

- The protocol has two fee systems: a taker-paid settlement fee, and a continuous fee on lender credit capped at 1% annualized.

- Both fees are currently off. On Ethereum, feeSetter() and feeClaimer() return 0x0 and defaultContinuousFee(USDC) = 0 (my reads on 2026-11-10).

Ethereum Midnight address: 0x471686c42792F93528B000beF54bC10E3aa2045f ([docs](https://docs.morpho.org/learn/concepts/midnight), [addresses](https://docs.morpho.org/get-started/resources/addresses/)).

- Distribution model: Morpho is mostly a back end. Coinbase, Robinhood, Ledger, Bitpanda, Kraken, Circle Mint, SoSoValue, Deel and others provide the front end and the user base, while Morpho provides the markets and liquidity.

#### Token mechanics and governance

- Voting happens on Snapshot (morpho.eth). Only MORPHO on Ethereum mainnet counts; legacy MORPHO must be wrapped to delegate (docs). A proposal needs 500k MORPHO to be submitted. Approved actions are executed by the governance multisig 0xcBa2…9AFa. The docs page still says 5/9, but on-chain the Safe now has 10 owners and a threshold of 6 (getOwners() / getThreshold(), 2026-11-10), matching MIP-133 (Spearbit added as signer, passed 2026-08-21).

- What governance controls (per docs):

- the treasury;

- the upgradeable wrapped-token contract;

- the Blue fee switch, up to 25% of borrower interest per market, plus the fee recipient;

- LLTV and IRM whitelisting;

- morpho.eth ENS.

- What the token does not have: no staking, ve-locking, buyback, burn or fee distribution exists today. Rewards are paid in MORPHO from the DAO treasury through Merkl (MIP-111) as liquidity incentives on new chains: Arbitrum, Unichain, Hyperliquid, Tempo (MIP-130), Arc (MIP-134 at $50k a month).

- Turnout is low and voting power is concentrated. Recent votes drew 2.5M–4.3M MORPHO "For" (MIP-131: 4.34M; MIP-134: 2.49M), about 0.25–0.6% of supply. Four Pillars (Mar 2026) found a single delegate, Stake Capital (8.8M votes), usually casts 73–82% of the votes on a proposal.

### Utility

- Real, current uses:

- governance votes over the narrow set of powers above, including the right to switch on fees and choose their recipient;

- collateral in a few Morpho markets (for example a MORPHO/USDC market on Ethereum);

- liquidity-mining reward currency.

- Not needed for: using the protocol (borrowers and lenders never touch MORPHO), paying fees, gas, insurance or backstop (no staking safety module like Aave's), or curating vaults.

- Conclusion: MORPHO is a governance-only token. Its economic value is a claim on an option: governance could one day route Blue and Midnight fees somewhere, and the Association is tied to the token because it owns Morpho Labs and has no shareholders. No cash-flow right exists on-chain.

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- A growth story with institutional distribution. Coinbase (BTC/ETH/XRP/SOL/PAXG loans; $3.4B collateral and $1.7B+ active loans as of 2026-11-06), Robinhood Chain ($1B deposits in about 3 months), Circle's Arc ($500M in about 2 weeks), Société Générale, Bitwise, Apollo, Ledger. Buyers treat MORPHO as an "index" on on-chain credit growth.

- Fee-switch option. 25% of about $210–300M a year in borrower interest would be roughly $50–75M a year, an estimate if switched on at the cap. Speculation on this recurs, although the Association advocates reinvesting instead.

- Strategic buyers.

- Apollo may acquire up to 90M MORPHO over 48 months from 2026-02-13. The token rose from $1.21 (2026-02-13) to $1.94 (2026-03-16) (CoinGecko daily closes).

- The $175M token raise of 2026-06-09 (Paradigm, a16z crypto, Ribbit; Apollo Funds, Circle Ventures, VanEck, Ledger Cathay and others) was priced at the monthly average price.

- A safety narrative after Aave's April 2026 bad debt. After the Kelp rsETH exploit, Aave's TVL fell from $26.3B to $14.6B (DefiLlama, 2026-04-15 → 2026-05-15). Morpho's CEO put Morpho's rsETH exposure at about $1M in two isolated markets.

- Fixed-rate credit, RWAs and tokenized stocks (Midnight, receivables financing, stock-backed loans) as new markets.

#### Sell pressure

- About 30% of supply is not yet circulating. Strategic-partner Cohort 3 vests linearly to Nov 2027; the founders' tranche may still be vesting (see above); the Association holds 90M with a mandate to spend it.

- DAO incentive emissions on Arc, Tempo and other new chains are paid in MORPHO and can be sold by farmers; the amount actually sold is not measured here.

- No yield or burn absorbs supply, so holding the token is purely a bet on price.

- Correlation with DeFi beta. The token fell 18.5% over 30 days despite record deposits, and October news flow was positive while the price slid from $2.69 (2026-10-07) to $2.00 (2026-11-10).

### The protocol/business

What it does: Morpho calls itself an "open credit network". It provides permissionless lending primitives (Blue, Midnight) and vault infrastructure (Vaults V2). Fintechs and institutions use it as their back end instead of building a lending stack. The Association reports 80+ contributors, up about 70% year on year, with offices in Paris, New York and Singapore ([2026-10-13](https://morpho.org/blog/morpho-appoints-coinbase-institutional-veteran-brian-foster-to-lead-next-phase-of-global-growth)).

#### Scale (DefiLlama, 2026-11-10)

| Metric | Value |

| --- | --- |

| TVL (collateral plus unborrowed supply) | $10.90B (re-read 2026-11-10; earlier read $10.92B = Blue $10.75B + Midnight $0.17B) |

| Active borrows | $5.70B (vs $3.50B on 2025-11-16, +63%) |

| TVL plus borrows ("deposits") | ~$16.6B; Morpho reported $17B+ ATH on 2026-10-13 |

| TVL by chain | Ethereum $4.95B; Base $4.22B; Robinhood Chain $0.61B; Arc $0.28B; Arbitrum $0.17B; Monad $0.17B; Hyperliquid L1 $0.13B; Pharos $0.12B; plus Tempo, Stable, Flare, World Chain, Katana, Unichain, OP |

| TVL history | $6.77B (2025-11-16) → $5.67B (2026-02-14) → $7.56B (2026-05-15) → $7.93B (2026-08-13) → $11.35B (2026-10-12) → $10.90B (2026-11-10) |

| Listed Blue markets (Morpho API) | 714 markets; loan-asset supply $6.34B (of which USDC $3.98B); borrow $5.52B |

| Vault TVL (listed, Morpho API) | V2 $5.49B (232 vaults); V1 $1.26B (94 vaults) |

| Midnight deposits | $200M+ (final week of Oct 2026, company newsletter 2026-11-05) |

