### How it works

#### The protocol (Euler V2, live since September 2024)

- Euler Vault Kit (EVK): anyone can deploy an ERC-4626 lending vault for one asset. The deployer chooses the oracle (Chainlink, Pyth, RedStone, Chronicle or custom adapters), the interest-rate model, supply and borrow caps, LTVs, liquidation settings, hooks and a governor. A vault with a governor is "managed" or "governed". A vault with the governor renounced is "ungoverned" or immutable. On Ethereum alone the factory 0x29a5…CC8e has deployed 902 vaults (on-chain getProxyListLength()).

- Ethereum Vault Connector (EVC, 0x0C9a…E383): the account layer. A borrow vault accepts selected other vaults as collateral. Users get 256 sub-accounts, batching (open a leveraged loop in one transaction), and "operators" that can manage positions on a user's behalf. EulerSwap and automation products are built on operators.

- Curators: third-party risk managers who govern vaults and earn an interest fee, a share of borrower interest set per vault, typically 10–15% and up to 30% on-chain. Examples are K3 Capital, Valos, Hyperithm, Clearstar, TelosC, Usual and Sentora. Sentora was the largest curator per Euler Labs in April 2026. EulerEarn is a MetaMorpho-style aggregator vault that allocates deposits across EVK vaults.

- Protocol fee: each vault's interest fee is split between the curator's feeReceiver and the protocol. The split is set in ProtocolConfig (Ethereum 0x4cD6…351b). The protocol share was 50% by default. It is 100% when a vault has no fee receiver, for example DAO vaults. Since 2026-05-12 the default protocol share is 0%, per the SetProtocolFeeShare(5000→0) event on Ethereum. The default also reads 0 on Avalanche, Monad, Base and BSC today. Some legacy per-vault overrides at 50% remain, mostly in stuck vaults (see section 7).

- Fee Flow (buyback): protocol fees, in the form of vault shares, were sent to a FeeFlowController (Ethereum 0xFcd3…1e32). It runs continuous Dutch auctions with a 14-day epoch: a buyer pays EUL and receives the whole fee inventory. The EUL went to the paymentReceiver, the DAO multisig 0xcAD001…. It was not burned. Fee Flow is turned off now, and getPrice() reads 0.

- Governance: EUL holders vote off-chain on Snapshot (eulerdao.eth), and multisigs execute. The Treasury Safe 0xC7C5… is Foundation-controlled. The Operational multisig 0xcAD001… is 4-of-8, with Labs and Foundation signers, and holds ProtocolConfig admin, the factory upgrade path, DAO-vault governor rights and bridging. There is also a Security Council multisig 0xb3b8…3c53. EVK factory upgrades go through a governor plus a timelock (0xfb03…A968). Note: the last Snapshot vote in eulerdao.eth closed on 2025-09-01 (EIP65 "Activate fees"). The 2026 decisions (sunsetting DAO markets, fees to zero) were posted on the forum and executed by multisig. No Snapshot vote was found (Snapshot GraphQL query, 2026-11-10).

- Liquidations: open to anyone. The discount scales with how far a position is underwater ("reverse Dutch auction" design inherited from V1). Euler Labs says it is releasing open-source liquidation bots.

#### How it works for users

A lender deposits, for example, USDC into a curated vault and earns the borrow rate times utilization, minus the curator fee. A borrower deposits collateral into a collateral vault, enables it via EVC, and borrows from a borrow vault that accepts that collateral. Looping (deposit, borrow, redeposit) is common. Datum Labs measured Euler's loans at 153.6% of net deposits in August 2026, the highest of 10 lenders. Institutions can use permissioned vaults with hooks, such as Keyring zk-KYC and Securitize tokenized securities.

### Utility

- Real, current utility: (1) governance voting and delegation (ERC20Votes) over the DAO treasury and protocol parameters. In practice execution sits with multisigs, and recent decisions skipped a token vote. (2) Incentive currency: rEUL rewards are distributed via Merkl.

- Dormant utility: the payment token for Fee Flow auctions, the only mechanism that created structural EUL demand. It has been inactive since April 2026.

- NOT needed for: using the protocol (supply, borrow, liquidate, deploy a vault, curate). It is not gas, not collateral that is required anywhere, not staked for security, and it has no ve-lock or fee share. No staking contract pays EUL holders.

- Verdict: today EUL is governance-only plus treasury optionality. Its economic link to protocol usage is switched off by governance.

### Why people buy and sell it

#### Demand drivers

- Fee-switch and buyback optionality: Fee Flow bought back EUL from 2024-09 to 2026-04, and the DAO can reinstate it. Its cumulative value is $3.20M (DefiLlama holders revenue) and its peak month was ~$720K (October 2025).

- "Modular lending infrastructure" narrative: Euler is pitched as the main alternative to Morpho. EUL market cap is $21.6M (2026-11-10) against MORPHO at $1.43B, so EUL trades as a deep-value or turnaround bet on that thesis.

- RWA and institutional narrative: Securitize permissioned vaults (June 2026), a VanEck tokenized fund on Euler (late May 2026, reported by CoinDesk), Laser Digital (Nomura) with Keyring fixed-income markets (announced 2026-09-02), Unlink privacy (June 2026), and Flare XRP yield (July 2026).

- Korean exchange listings: the Upbit KRW listing on 2026-07-26 took the price from $1.09 (7/25) to $2.29 (7/27). Volume rose from ~$3M/day to $178M/day (CoinGecko daily data), and the move faded to $1.20–1.40 within weeks. Upbit is the #2 EUL venue by 24h volume today. Bithumb also lists EUL/KRW.

- Other listings: Binance (USDT, TRY, USDC), Coinbase, Kraken, KuCoin, MEXC and Crypto.com, plus Uniswap v3/v4 liquidity.

#### Supply and sell pressure

- No value accrual: protocol revenue is ~$81/day (DefiLlama) and nothing goes to holders, which removes the fundamental bid.

- Collapse in usage: net TVL fell from $1.69B (2025-10-01; peak $2.20B on 2025-10-06) to $252.7M (2026-11-10), and Ethereum-chain borrowing fell from $787M (2026-01-01) to $34M (DefiLlama).

- Fee Flow buyers were sellers: auction buyers bought EUL in order to pay it in. The EUL ended up in DAO multisigs (~0.69M EUL moved from the Operational multisig to Safe 0x0e33 on 2026-10-02; that Safe has a history of paying EUL out) and could be redeployed.

- Potential inflation: the 2.718% mint (~739K EUL) has been callable since 2026-01-01.

- rEUL farming: early redeemers forfeit 80% to the burn address, which is small in absolute terms (rEUL supply is 90.7K).

- Large unlabeled holders: three EOAs hold 20% of supply and move it frequently. Exchange-like wallets with high turnover can add supply to the market quickly.

- Thin liquidity: 24h volume is $0.6–1.1M on most days in November 2026, so modest sales move the price. The price fell from $1.23 (10/25) to $0.90 (11/10).

### The protocol/business

#### What it does and product lines

- Euler Lending (EVK + EVC): permissionless, modular lending markets. Isolated pairs, clusters and cross-collateral setups are all possible.

- Euler Earn: curated yield aggregator vaults. A redeploy is planned to add loss socialization and withdrawal-run controls (Q1 2026 retrospective).

- Permissioned markets: Keyring zkKYC clusters, Securitize permissioned ERC-4626 vaults for tokenized securities, and the Laser Digital/Keyring fixed-income markets announced 2026-09-02 (no launch date, capital or fees disclosed).

- EulerSwap: credit-backed AMM launched in 2025. Labs says it "couldn't be sustained" and that a mature version is shelved until the infrastructure is ready.

- Lite app, SDK and data APIs: new backend and SDK being shipped in 2026. The old app is being replaced.

- Strategic identity (April 2026): "Infrastructure. Period." Labs exits running markets. The DAO-managed markets were sunset or handed to K3 Capital and AlphaGrowth (forum, 2026-04-10). Gauntlet confirmed handover of its curated vaults on 2026-04-28.

#### Audience and where it is used

- Users: DeFi-native yield farmers and loopers (PT/Pendle, Ethena-style carry, stablecoin loops), curators and risk managers, and increasingly institutions and RWA issuers via permissioned vaults.

- Chains (DefiLlama net TVL, 2026-11-10):

- Monad $195.9M (largest, 77.5% of net TVL)

- Ethereum $34.6M

- Base $10.6M

- Avalanche $4.3M

- Plasma $2.3M

- Unichain $1.6M

